  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 AM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nA broad ridge holds over the southern Plains early this morning\nwith weak westerlies over Kansas, while the northern stream trough\nremains well north of the region and has shifted east over\nManitoba and Ontario. At the surface, broad high pressure sits\nover the Kansas, Missouri, and Oklahoma border area, and a weak\nfront has already brought a wind shift to north central Kansas\nwith drier air behind it. Monday's highs reached the mid 70s, a\nfew degrees above the NBM, as deep mixing dropped afternoon\ndewpoints into the upper 30s. That deep mixing is the main reason\nfog is even less of a concern than Monday morning, when it stayed\nconfined to a few low spots. Crossover temps sit in the upper 30s\nto around 40 with temperatures still in the 40s, the VWP shows 20\nto 25 kt between 925 and 850 mb keeping the boundary layer at\nleast partially mixed, and the drier air arriving from the north\ncuts the moisture further. Forecast soundings carry little to no\nsaturation depth, so at most patchy shallow fog forms in the river\nvalleys toward sunrise.\n\nA weak and diffuse boundary stalls near the I-70 corridor today,\nwith light east to northeast winds early turning southerly as the\nday warms. It holds north central highs in the mid 60s while the\nrest of the area reaches the upper 60s to near 70. The NBM ran\nabout 10 degrees too moist on afternoon dewpoints Monday, and with\na similar airmass and deep mixing expected, dewpoints likely end\nup below guidance again this afternoon. A Pacific trough digs into\nthe upper Mississippi Valley tonight, and the height falls and\ndeepening northwest flow behind it drive a stronger cold front\nthrough the area around sunrise Wednesday, with northwest winds\ngusting 25 to 30 mph in the afternoon and highs in the low 60s.\nMixing heights near 4500 ft behind the front support another round\nof deep mixing, so minimum RH likely falls into the upper 20s to\nmid 30s, but with 10 hour fuel moisture near 20 percent, above the\nrange where fire danger climbs meaningfully, fire danger stays low\ndespite the wind.\n\nBehind the front the trough settles over the eastern CONUS with\nthe ridge holding over the western CONUS, leaving Kansas in\nnorthwest flow as H85 temperatures fall from near 11 C Wednesday\nto near 1 C by Friday. Guidance has converged since last night,\nwith the GEPS warm tail gone and the NBM now matching the ensemble\nmedian, so Friday is no longer a two scenario day. Highs hold in\nthe low 50s Thursday and upper 40s Friday, and lows in the upper\n20s to around 30 Friday and Saturday mornings bring frost and a\nfreeze to most of the area after a growing season that has run\nlong this fall without a hard freeze.\n\nRidging builds back aloft over the weekend with highs rebounding\ninto the 50s, though the GEPS runs warmer than the ECMWF ensemble\nfor Saturday and the speed of the warm up is now the open\nquestion. A southern stream system crosses Texas and the southern\nPlains Saturday night into Sunday, with roughly three quarters of\nthe ensemble clusters tracking it south of the area, so rain\nchances stay low and fade Sunday night with dry weather to start\nnext week.",
